South Africa is the only nation in  history to unilaterally disarm its own  nuclear weapons others have relinquished  theirs like Ukraine did but those were  Soviet weapons they inherited and not  their own home developed ones given that  no other nation has ever developed such  powerful weapons and then just decided  they didn't want them anymore this  raises the question why did South Africa  give up its nukes so first of all a bit  of background on South Africa by the  1970 South Africa's global position was  complicated the country was run by its  white minority in its government  introduced a social system known as  apartheid which means separation the  system restricted the black majority's  freedoms in the country these policies  towards the black majority in South  Africa meant that his relations with its  neighboring countries weren't exactly  great with the sole exception of  Rhodesia obviously as for its broader  international relations these changed  over time its relationship with the USSR  and the Communist world was always  terrible whereas its relations with the  USA in the UK were pretty good in the  1960s but as time went on they soured  early on the u.s. also had laws which  enforced segregation but when these laws  were repealed it became increasingly  less acceptable to maintain good  diplomatic relations with South Africa  and by the 1970s South Africa was  largely isolated things were made worse  when Portugal who had been covertly  supportive of South Africa withdrew from  its African colonies leaving it even  more isolated and it was this isolation  which led to South Africa developing its  own nuclear weapons although it took a  long time for the government to come to  this conclusion its thinking was that if  its survival were to be threatened that  none in the international community  would help it thus the only surefire way  of guaranteeing that the current South  Africa would see tomorrow was to build a  nuclear arsenal the mere existence of  which would deter aggressors so South  Africa had had its own nuclear program  since 1948 but this was devoted to  nuclear energy making economic use of  the country's abundant uranium deposits  in 1965 of the help of the USA it  commissioned its first nuclear reactor  and in 1970 Prime Minister John Vorster  authorized the creation of the uranium  enrichment facility although he stressed  that this would only be for peaceful  purposes exactly zero people believed  him  in the late 1970s events to the north in  Angola change government policy you see  Angola was undergoing a civil war which  had seen the intervention of Cuban  forces which were interned back by the  USSR South Africa saw this as a direct  threat to its very existence and so this  was when Prime Minister Peter Botha push  to develop nuclear weapons South  Africa's first nuclear weapon was  finished in November 1970  9 so by 1989 South Africa had seven  nuclear bombs of about the same  sophistication and yield as the bomb  dropped on Hiroshima an election in the  same year so Frederic de Klerk elected  as president in his priorities were to  win South Africa's international  isolation which would be done via  internal reforms to be blunt apartheid  days were numbered and it was his job to  make sure the transition away from it  wasn't to Civil War II this transition  would then bring South Africa back into  the international community which was  more important now that the Cold War was  over  you see prior to this the West could  tolerate apartheid South Africa because  it was fiercely anti-communist but now  the Warsaw Pact was dead the leverage  was gone there was also one other reason  you see with tensions running high the  white minority was worried about what  the next majority chosen government  would look like to simplify further the  white minority didn't trust a black  government with nuclear weapons and so  got rid of them with this inevitable  change coming - clerk thought the  rehabilitating South Africa would be  easier if his nuclear weapon program was  ended and in 1990 he did just that and  within a year all seven weapons had been  decommissioned two years later he  announced to the world that South Africa  had previously had nuclear weapons but  had also gotten rid of them South Africa  had just made history I hope you enjoyed  this episode and thank you for watching  with extra thanks to these patrons you  can see on screen for their continued  support and an extra special thanks to  James Bissonnette David archaeologists  as ARCA Flash Danny Maloney party-boy  Koch coolin Castleman Rob Waterhouse  Chris wicker Gustav Swan David Silverman  Winston Heywood Christian checky Maggie  packs Kowski Lexi Schwinn Anthony  Beckett Robert Wetzel Skye Chappell Icke  spinning three plates are on the white  and Michael Reynolds
 

